"use strict";

/* generated from story/scenes/act5_ch29.md — do not edit */

SC["a5c29_gather"]={img:"threads",
 t:()=>SYS(`CHAPTER TWENTY-NINE — GATHERING THE THREADS`)+P(`The counter-pattern's last requirement is the one Suyin saved for the end, because it is the one that was never engineering.`)+P(`“The Shuttle holds the center,” she says, at the model warp, “and the rota holds the load. But a pattern is not its tension, stitch: it's its ANCHORS. The old Loom anchors in bedrock and dead weight. Yours anchors in the only thing that holds better: thread freely given. Knots tied for their own sake, before anyone knew they'd be load-bearing.” She looks at you over the forty lines. “You've been weaving the anchor-ring for three acts, child. Go collect it.”`)+P(`So the last days before the crossover become a procession, because word goes out: the weaver needs what the weaver is owed — and the river answers.`)+((G.mei>=3)?(P(`Mei comes first, of course, because she was always going to be first and would deny it under torture. She holds out her wrist, where a thread of your gold has ridden since a poultice in a tournament infirmary. “Take the knot,” she says. “Not a new one. THAT one. It's already load-bearing: ask anyone who's watched you eat when I'm not there.”`)):(""))+((G.bao>=3)?(P(`Bao brings his thread tied around a steamed bun, because of course he does. “The first day on the river,” he says, “you split your meal with the firewood boy. Everything since is interest.” He is crying and grinning and neither one yields. “Tie me in where the weight is, brother. I've been carrying things up mountains my whole life. It turns out it was training.”`)):(""))+((G.sho>=3)?(P(`Granny Sho does not bring her thread; she IS it. She sets her palm against the model warp and the resonance the recruiters wrote about forty years ago answers your gold like a struck bell finally allowed its note. “Forty years I listened at the bottom of the stairs,” she says. “Tie me where I can HEAR it hold.”`)):(""))+((G.yan>=3)?(P(`Yan Shuo presents his formally, as is right: a thread wound around the hilt-cord of his sword, offered with both hands and the deep tournament bow. “Honor is a rota too,” he says. “It always was. I merely mistook whose. The Azure Cloud stands its watch in your pattern, weaver. First shift.”`)):(""))+((G.tang>=2)?(P(`Auntie Tang's thread arrives by courier, tied around a ladle, with a note in cleaver-stroke characters: <i>The basement holds up the mountain. Always did. Put us in the sky too, dear, and MIND YOUR SOUP. — T.</i>`)):(""))+((G.f.ruan>=2)?(P(`Ruan's is measured to the inch, docketed, and tied with a bookkeeper's knot that you have to admire for a full minute before you understand it: it is the knot from his master's ledger-bindings, re-tied MIRROR-WISE. “The measuring rod,” he says, “measures for something now. Enter it in the assets column, brother. Final entry. The books balance.”`)):(""))+((G.f.a3_feral=="calmed")?(P(`And up the lake, poled by two marsh-men who will not take payment, comes a reed boat carrying the Frayed One — consignment forty-one, the Reed Wife, the other foreigner — wrapped in the quiet you wove her and carrying it like a lamp. She says nothing; speech still costs her. She takes your hand, puts it against the silence in her chest where the screaming was, and PUSHES: the whole mended quiet of her, offered as anchor. The first foreign soul the Court ever dropped, holding up the new sky. The Between's needlework, completing itself.`)):(""))+((!G.f.a1_su_sold)?(P(`Little Su sends hers from the mountain sewn inside a letter she wrote herself: the kitchens taught her letters last winter, the letter says, mostly so she could write this one: <i>You paid a debt for me when I was a tray-girl. The thread is from my first apron. It is small but it has never once broken.</i>`)):(""))+((G.f.a2_lin_friend || G.f.a2_grace)?(P(`The tournament sends threads too: Lin's arrives knotted in flowing style with a challenge written on the tag — <i>teach me THIS footwork next</i> — and Hou's in iron style, blunt as its sender: <i>you walked me through where I cracked, kitchen-school. Walk the sky through it too.</i>`)):(""))+((G.f.a3_documented)?(P(`The abbot of Clearwater comes in person, older than his robes, carrying forty years of receipts and the tenth bracket's memory like stones in both sleeves. “You watched the cost with me,” he says. “Let an old coward hold one line of the better answer.” His thread is mulberry-paper, twisted; it holds like wire.`)):(""))+((G.f.a4_xian=="turned")?(P(`And Madam Xian brings the rota's master-line itself — four hundred willing tithes braided into one white cable, hers at the core — and presents it with a hostess's bow and a convert's ferocity. “The Court's column,” she says, “correctly filed at last.”`)):(""))+P(`The seven sleepers from the Cocoon House send theirs together, braided, from four different skies: freed souls, every one a suture heaven sent to this world's tearing, every one finally allowed to do the work it was sent for.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`And Suyin ties hers last, where the ring closes.`,do:()=>{G.f.a5_threads=1+(G.mei>=3?1:0)+(G.bao>=3?1:0)+(G.sho>=3?1:0)+(G.yan>=3?1:0)+(G.tang>=2?1:0)+((G.f.ruan||0)>=2?1:0)+(G.f.a3_feral=="calmed"?1:0)+(!G.f.a1_su_sold?1:0)+((G.f.a2_lin_friend||G.f.a2_grace)?1:0)+(G.f.a3_documented?1:0)+(G.f.a4_xian=="turned"?1:0)+1;},go:"a5c29_ring"}]};

SC["a5c29_ring"]={t:()=>P(`The anchor-ring rises in the warp-gallery over one long afternoon, and you stop work to look at it, because for once there is nothing in it that needs fixing:`)+P(`A ring of knots, hanging in the cathedral light above the model warp: your gold running through every one, and every one a different hand. The herbalist's square knot and the firewood boy's burly hitch; a blind woman's resonance, which is not a knot at all but a held note that the gold winds around; a swordsman's formal lashing, a cook's ladle-loop, a bookkeeper's mirror-bind. Marsh-quiet and apron-thread and mulberry wire. Each one wrong by every other one's grammar. All of them holding, BECAUSE the grammars differ: load coming onto the ring from any direction finds some knot tied exactly against it. No two the same; nothing interchangeable; nothing harvestable. A pattern the Court could never have built, because the Court files people by what they yield, and this ring is made entirely of what people GIVE.`)+P(`${G.f.a5_threads} threads answer. Suyin counts them twice, hands still, and says, finally, in silk worn down to plain cotton: “Forty years I held a province alone, and the books called it strength. THIS is what strength was supposed to look like. I'm glad I lived wrong long enough to see it.”`)+SYS(`The anchor-ring holds: ${G.f.a5_threads} freely-given threads. Every one will bear into the final pattern, and every one will be standing in the gallery when the sky changes hands.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`The last night before the crossover.`,go:"a5c29_night"}]};

SC["a5c29_night"]={t:()=>P(`No one calls it a vigil, and everyone treats it like one.`)+P(`The rota makes camp through the warp-galleries — four hundred willing shoulders sleeping in shifts that Yan has drilled into them like sword-forms — and your own people gather at the model warp the way families gather in kitchens: drawn by warmth nobody planned. Tea happens.`)+((G.bao>=3)?(P(`Bao produces, from nowhere anyone can explain, honest mountain bean cakes, and distributes them with the gravity of a quartermaster arming troops.`)):(""))+((G.mei>=3)?(P(`Mei checks everyone's pulse by holding their wrists a moment too long while pretending to pass cups, and thinks no one notices, and everyone notices, and no one says.`)):(""))+((G.sho>=3)?(P(`Granny Sho and Suyin sit shoulder to shoulder against a piling, talking in courtyard-voices about people sixty years gone, and their laughter — two old women, rusty and identical — does more for the rota's nerve than any speech.`)):(""))+P(`Tomorrow the season turns. Tomorrow the old Loom stands down after four hundred years, a gap opens in the middle of a held sky, and a boy from a bus stop ties the largest knot ever attempted by anyone. Tonight there is tea, and the city's tide of thread breathing through the dark, and the specific human arithmetic of the people who came when you needed a ring.`)+((G.f.bg=="sister")?(P(`You sit at the camp's edge a while with your hand over the line in your chest, the one that runs off the world's hem to a porch light, and you make it the only promise that has ever mattered to either of you: home, or a sky worth not coming home for. Possibly both. You're a Pattern Sage now. The books say both is a legend; the books said that about you.`)):("")),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Sleep comes easier than it has any right to. The crossover at dawn.`,go:"a5c30_morning"}]};